## Read both series, not just the spike

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The received series tells when reports reached support. The merged series tells when the desk filed them together.
Use received dates to discuss incident history; do not date the start from the bulk merger.

## Triage rule: same cause, not same subject

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) was accepted on 11 June 2024.
It is kept unchanged as a historical record, but its subject-only merge rule is superseded by
[decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause).
Its old review date warrants rechecking, not automatic acceptance of the prose.
The runnable explorer already distinguishes causes and disagrees with that old rule.

<DedupeExplorer />

Different subjects can describe the same cause. Matching subjects can describe different causes because
the tenant's form or the intake channel differs. With no tenant on file, the classifier returns no cause:
keep the report unplaced and investigate; two unknown causes are not a duplicate match.
Read the body too, because a tenant-based classifier cannot prove that every symptom has that cause.

## Next desk actions

- Link new reports to the correct problem ticket only after checking tenant, channel, form and symptoms.
- Keep report IDs unique; overlapping export pages are not extra affected users.
- Keep anomalous symptoms visible and request the evidence listed above. Do not invent a timezone diagnosis.
- Track parser and hint dispositions separately: completed for the reported parser fix, not planned for the retired form.
- Before claiming deployment verification, obtain reproduction and regression evidence; this review only has the desk's status report.
- Refresh this snapshot against current desk data before making a live-status claim.

